{"id":"9120a70b-6ec6-42ca-9044-eb6bddef81a5","object":"chat.completion","created":1784988539,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import sys\\n    # Validate required keys\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must contain 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'nodes' must be a list\\\")\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    if not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'required_symbols' must be a list\\\")\\n    \\n    # Build id->node map and children map, validate fields\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each node must be a dict\\\")\\n        nid = node.get('id')\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node 'id' must be a string\\\")\\n        if nid in node_by_id:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"duplicate node id: {nid}\\\")\\n        # validate other fields\\n        for key in ('parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility'):\\n            if key not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"node {nid} missing key {key}\\\")\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None and not isinstance(parent, str):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"node {nid} parent must be string or None\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['symbol'], str):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"node {nid} symbol must be string\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['depth'], int):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"node {nid} depth must be int\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['removable'], bool):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"node {nid} removable must be bool\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['utility'], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"node {nid} utility must be numeric\\\")\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n        # set up children map\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            # root parent None\\n            pass\\n    \\n    # Validate parents exist (except None)\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None and pid not in node_by_id:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"node {node['id']} references unknown parent {pid}\\\")\\n    \\n    # Validate required_symbols are strings\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if not isinstance(sym, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"required_symbols must be strings\\\")\\n    \\n    # Initialize symbol counts\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    \\n    # Check that all required symbols are present initially\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"required symbol '{sym}' not present in any node\\\")\\n    \\n    # Group nodes by depth for processing\\n    depth_groups = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        d = node['depth']\\n        depth_groups.setdefault(d, []).append(node['id'])\\n    # Sort depths descending\\n    sorted_depths = sorted(depth_groups.keys(), reverse=True)\\n    # Within each depth, sort by id for stable tie break\\n    for d in depth_groups:\\n        depth_groups[d].sort()\\n    \\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    # Copy current symbol counts for simulation\\n    current_counts = dict(symbol_counts)\\n    \\n    for depth in sorted_depths:\\n        for nid in depth_groups[depth]:\\n            node = node_by_id[nid]\\n            if not node['removable']:\\n                continue\\n            # Check children retained: all children must already be removed\\n            children_removed = True\\n            for cid in children.get(nid, []):\\n                if cid not in removed_set:\\n                    children_removed = False\\n                    break\\n            if not children_removed:\\n                continue\\n            # Check required symbols: simulate removal\\n            sym = node['symbol']\\n            # Check that after decrement, all required symbols have count > 0\\n            ok = True\\n            for req_sym in required_symbols:\\n                cnt = current_counts.get(req_sym, 0)\\n                if req_sym == sym:\\n                    cnt -= 1\\n                if cnt <= 0:\\n                    ok = False\\n                    break\\n            if not ok:\\n                continue\\n            # Remove node\\n            removed_set.add(nid)\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n            current_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    \\n    # Retained nodes: all not removed, sorted by id\\n    retained_ids = sorted([nid for nid in node_by_id if nid not in removed_set])\\n    \\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) that implements a grammar-guided node reduction algorithm. The task is SE-PE-01. The payload contains a tree of nodes, each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. There is required_symbols list. The goal: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving that all required_symbols are still present in the remaining tree. The algorithm: consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (i.e., post-order traversal). For each candidate node, we tentatively remove it if: it's removable, all required symbols are still represented after removal (i.e., every required symbol appears in the remaining nodes), and its removal is grammar-valid? Actually \"grammar_valid\" is mentioned but not defined. From context, it might mean that after removal, the tree remains a valid parse tree? But we don't have a grammar. Possibly it means that we cannot remove a node if it would break the parent-child relationships? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So the basic rule: we can remove a removable node if it is not required (its symbol not in required_symbols), and after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere. Also, we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (non-removed). Actually \"a node with retained children\" means if any descendant will remain, you cannot remove the node because that would orphan them? But if we remove a node, its children become children of its parent? In tree reduction, often removal means the node is deleted and its children are reattached to the parent. But the description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\". Could be that we only remove leaf nodes? But nodes can have children. Another clue: the fixture has nodes with depth, and some nodes are not removable. The root is not removable. The required_symbols are \"root\" and \"s1\". The nodes with symbol \"s1\" appear at depth 1 and depth 2. Those might be required and not removable. Indeed, node n1 (symbol s1, depth 1) has removable false. Node n4 (symbol s1, depth 2) has removable true. So s1 appears twice, but only one is required? Wait, required_symbols is list of symbols that must be present in the final tree. So the final tree must contain at least one node with symbol \"root\" and at least one node with symbol \"s1\". So we can remove some nodes with s1 as long as at least one remains.\n\nThe algorithm: we have to produce two lists: removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). So effectively, we decide which nodes to remove. The order: consider deeper before shallower (post-order). Among same depth, use node ID as tie-breaker (probably string comparison). For each node in that order, if it is removable, and its removal would still keep all required symbols present, and it has no children that are retained (i.e., all its descendants are also removed? That would be necessary because if we remove the parent, children become orphaned? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means if a node has children that are not removed, we cannot remove it. So we can only remove a node if all its children are also removed (or it has no children). So removal proceeds bottom-up: we remove leaf removable nodes first, then possibly their parents if all children are gone.\n\nAlso, we need to consider the \"grammar_valid\" condition: maybe after removal, the tree must still be a valid parse tree according to grammar. But we don't have grammar rules. Perhaps it's just that the tree structure must remain a tree (no cycles, etc.) and that's automatically satisfied by removing nodes and reattaching children? Or maybe the removal operation is simply deleting the node and its entire subtree? The phrase \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" suggests that we can delete nodes and replace with something? But the output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. So it's just deletion.\n\nGiven ambiguity, we should base on the provided fixtures. Let's simulate for the first case:\n\nCase payload:\nnodes: (I'll list them)\n- root: depth 0, removable false, symbol root\n- n1: parent root, depth1, removable false, symbol s1\n- n2: parent root, depth1, removable true, symbol s2\n- n3: parent root, depth1, removable false, symbol s0\n- n4: parent n2, depth2, removable true, symbol s1\n- n5: parent n3, depth2, removable false, symbol s2\n- n6: parent n1, depth2, removable true, symbol s0\n\nrequired_symbols: [root, s1]\n\nWe need to decide removals. Start with deepest nodes (depth2): n4, n5, n6. Order among depth2 by ID: n4 < n5 < n6? Assuming lexicographic: \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\". So n4 first. n4 is removable, symbol s1. If we remove n4, we lose one s1, but n1 also has s1, so required symbols (root and s1) still present (root node, n1). Also n4 has no children? It is leaf. So condition passes. So tentatively remove n4. Then next: n5 (removable false) -> skip. Next: n6 (removable true, symbol s0). After removal, required symbols ok. n6 leaf. So remove n6. Now move to depth1 nodes: n1, n2, n3. Order by ID: n1, n2, n3. n1: removable false -> skip. n2: removable true, symbol s2. Check required symbols: root, s1 remain? root yes, s1: after removing n4, n1 still has s1. So required ok. But does n2 have any retained children? n2's children: n4 was removed, so no retained children. So condition passes. Remove n2. Then n3: removable false -> skip. Depth0 root: not removable. So final removed: [n4, n6, n2] in that order? Actually removal order is the order we process and decide to remove: n4, n6, n2. But the order specified \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So order among same depth is sorted by ID. So for depth2 we processed n4 then n5(nope) then n6. So removal order: n4, n6, then n2. That seems plausible.\n\nRetained nodes: all nodes not removed: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted by ID: n1, n3, n5, root? Sorting string IDs: \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\". That's plausible.\n\nNow check second fixture: similar structure but different utility values. But utility is only for evaluating (maybe interestingness)? The algorithm description mentions \"utility\" but no explicit formula. Possibly utility is used to decide which nodes to remove if multiple candidates? But the algorithm says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" No mention of utility. However, the \"interestingness oracle\" might be that the removal should preserve some property? The root and required symbols are fixed. The interestingness might be that the reduced tree still \"makes sense\"? But we have no grammar. Possibly the intended algorithm is to greedily remove removable nodes that are not required, in order of decreasing depth, and if a node's removal would cause a required symbol to be missing, skip. That's what we did.\n\nBut note: in the second fixture, n4 is symbol s1 and removable true. There are two s1 nodes: n1 (depth1, not removable) and n4 (depth2, removable). If we remove n4, we still have n1 with s1. So removal should happen. Similarly, n2 removable true, its child n4 is removed, so n2 can be removed. n6 removable true, leaf, can be removed. So same removals.\n\nThird fixture same pattern.\n\nThus, the algorithm seems consistent.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) that does:\n\n- Validate input structure (has 'nodes' and 'required_symbols').\n- Build a mapping from node id to node dict.\n- Build mapping from parent to children.\n- Determine which nodes are initially present (all).\n- Perform post-order traversal: depth decreasing, within same depth sort by id.\n- For each node in that order:\n   - If node is removable (removable == True) and its symbol not in required_symbols? Actually removable flag is separate. The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we must also check that its symbol is not in required_symbols? But the required_symbols is a list of symbols that must be represented. A node with a required symbol could be removed if there is another node with same symbol that remains. So we should not block removal based on symbol alone; we block only if after removal, the set of symbols in remaining nodes would be missing a required symbol. So we can remove a node with a required symbol as long as another node with that symbol exists. E.g., n4 has symbol s1 but n1 also has s1, so removal allowed.\n   - Also check that node has no children that are still in retained set (i.e., not removed). We'll maintain a set of removed nodes, initially empty. When processing, we consider the current set of removed nodes; for a node to be removed, all its descendants must already be removed (since we process deepest first, we can check that all children are in removed set or will be removed? Actually we process in post-order, so when we consider a node, its children have already been considered. If a child was not removed (either because not removable or not eligible), then that child is retained. So the node cannot be removed if any child is retained. So we check: for each child of this node, if child id not in removed set, then retain child -> cannot remove node.\n   - Then check that after hypothetical removal, all required symbols are still present. We can compute: for each required symbol, there exists at least one node (not including this node) in the original set that has that symbol and is not removed (and also not this node). Since we haven't removed this node yet, we need to check if there is another node with that symbol besides this one that is not removed. We can precompute a mapping of symbol to list of node ids. Then for each required symbol, ensure that after removing this node, the count of nodes with that symbol (excluding this node) that are not removed is > 0. But careful: other nodes with that symbol might be removed already. So we need to maintain a current remaining count per symbol.\n   - If all conditions pass, add node to removed set and record in removal order.\n\n- After processing all nodes, produce retained node ids: all node ids not in removed set, sorted.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained_ids}.\n\nEdge cases: root not removable, so never removed. If required_symbols not satisfied initially? The tree always includes root and required symbols. Could be that a required symbol appears only on a removable node, and that node's removal would cause loss; then we cannot remove it. That's fine.\n\nAlso note: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So when ordering nodes at same depth, sort by ID (string). That determines processing order.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: it's a dict with 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list.\n2. Build dict node_by_id: {id: node}\n3. Build list of all nodes.\n4. Build children mapping: {parent_id: list of child_ids}.\n5. Compute initial symbol counts: symbol_counts = defaultdict(int) for each node's symbol.\n6. Sort nodes for traversal: group by depth, then sort each group by id. But we want descending depth. So get all unique depths, sort descending, for each depth get list of node ids at that depth sorted by id.\n7. Initialize removed set and removed_order list.\n8. For each depth in descending order:\n   For each node_id in sorted node ids at that depth:\n       node = node_by_id[node_id]\n       if not node['removable']: continue\n       # check children retained: for each child in children_mapping.get(node_id, []):\n           if child not in removed_set: -> cannot remove, skip.\n       # check required symbols representation:\n       # simulate removal: decrement count for this node's symbol, then check all required symbols have count >0. But we need to consider other removed nodes already. So we maintain current symbol_counts that reflect remaining nodes after removals. Initially symbol_counts is from all nodes. When we remove a node, we decrement its symbol. So before removing, we have current counts. For checking, we need to see if after decrement, all required counts >0. So:\n           req_ok = True\n           for req_sym in required_symbols:\n               if req_sym == node['symbol']:\n                   if symbol_counts[req_sym] - 1 <= 0:\n                       # but there might be other nodes with same symbol? Actually symbol_counts[req_sym] already includes this node. So if symbol_counts[req_sym] - 1 == 0, that means this node is the only remaining one.\n                       req_ok = False\n                       break\n               else:\n                   if symbol_counts[req_sym] <= 0: # should not happen initially\n                       req_ok = False\n                       break\n       # If both conditions ok:\n           removed_set.add(node_id)\n           removed_order.append(node_id)\n           symbol_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1\n\n9. After processing all nodes, retained = [id for id in node_by_id if id not in removed_set], sort by string (default Python sort).\n10. Return dict.\n\nCheck first case: initial symbol_counts: root:1, s1:2 (n1,n4), s2:2 (n2,n5), s0:2 (n3,n6). Process depth2: n4 (symbol s1, count 2-> after removal 1, required s1 count 1>0 ok). n5 not removable. n6 (symbol s0, count 2->1, required symbols root and s1 unaffected). Then depth1: n1 not removable, n2 (symbol s2, children n4 removed, so children retained check: n4 in removed set, ok. After removal, symbol s2 count from 2 to 1, required symbols ok). n3 not removable. So removed = [n4,n6,n2], retained sorted = [n1,n3,n5,root]. That matches expectation.\n\nNow we must implement in Python, no external libs, no mutation of input. Use only standard lib.\n\nWrite solve(case) function.\n\nAlso need to handle possible invalid cases: check that nodes list is present, required_symbols list, each node has required fields. Raise ValueError if missing or invalid. But the description says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should validate.\n\nImplementation plan for validation:\n- case is a dict; check 'nodes' key exists and is list; check 'required_symbols' key exists and is list.\n- For each node, check it has 'id', 'parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility'. Utility may not be used but should be present. Type checks: id string, parent string or None, symbol string, depth int, removable bool, utility float.\n- Ensure root node has parent = None.\n- Check that all referenced parent ids exist (except None).\n- Check no duplicate ids.\n- Check that required_symbols are strings.\n- Possibly check that required symbols are all present in at least one node initially.\n\nBut the fixture payloads are well-formed; we can implement minimal validation. We'll do basic checks.\n\nWe'll output the Python source as a JSON string.\n\nNow, produce the final response in the required format: entire response is a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is a string containing Python source. No markdown, no extra text.\n\nI'll write the Python code carefully.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The order of processing among nodes of same depth but with different parents: we just sort by id. That's correct.\n\nEdge: removable nodes that are required symbol but have duplicate? Already handled.\n\nNow, write code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":4811,"total_tokens":6980,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3574},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}